{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Its pots.</b> Like cauldrons." "<b>To remove its ashes.</b> To remove its ashes into them — and this is how Onkelos renders it: לְמִסְפֵּי קִטְמֵהּ — to sweep the ashes into them. For there are words in the Hebrew language where one root alternates between building and destroying, as in ״וַתַּשְׁרֵשׁ שָׁרָשֶׁיהָ״ (Psalms 80), ״אֱוִיל מַשְׁרִישׁ״ (Job 5), and its opposite: ״בְּכָל תְּבוּאָתִי תְשָׁרֵשׁ״ (Job 31:12); and similar: ״בִּסְעִפֶּיהָ פֹּרִיָּה״ (Isaiah 17:6), and its opposite: ״מְסָעֵף פֻּארָה״ (Isaiah 10:33) — splitting its branches; and similar: ״וְזֶה הָאַחֲרוֹן עִצְּמוֹ״ (Jeremiah 50) — broke his bones; and similar: ״וַיִּסְקְלֻהוּ בָאֲבָנִים״ (I Kings 21:13), and its opposite: ״סַקְּלוּ מֵאֶבֶן״ (Isaiah 62:10) — removed its stones, and likewise ״וַיְעַזְּקֵהוּ וַיְסַקְּלֵהוּ״ (ibid. 5). So too here: לְדַשְּׁנוֹ — to remove its ashes; וּבְלַעַז אדשצנדר״יר." "<b>Its shovels.</b> As its Targum [renders] — scoops with which one takes the ashes; they are like a pot-cover of thin metal with a handle; וּבְלַעַז ווד״יל." "<b>Its basins.</b> To receive in them the blood of the offerings." "<b>Its forks.</b> Like curved hooks; one strikes them into the flesh and they pierce into it, and one turns the coals of the arrangement with them so that their burning is hastened; ובְלַעַז קרוצי״נש, and in the language of the Sages: צִנּוֹרִיּוֹת (Yoma 12)." "<b>And its pans.</b> They have a receptacle for taking coals from the altar with them, to carry them onto the inner altar for the incense offering. And by reason of their scooping they are called מַחְתּוֹת, as in ״לַחְתּוֹת אֵשׁ מִיָּקוּד״ (Isaiah 30) — a term for drawing fire from its place; and so ״הֲיַחְתֶּה אִישׁ אֵשׁ בְּחֵיקוֹ״ (Proverbs 6)." "<b>For all its vessels.</b> Like כָּל כֵּילָיו — 'all its vessels.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {1 "The la'az is Old French escindier/eschandir, 'to remove ashes' (a denominative verb from esciende/achandelle, 'ashes'). Rashi's point is that the Hebrew לדשנו can mean either 'to make ashes' or 'to remove ashes,' like other Hebrew roots that carry both active and privative senses.", 2 "The la'az is Old French wadel/wadille, attested in Rashi's usage as 'shovel' or 'scoop' (cf. Modern French houe). The point of the analogy to a pot-cover (כיסוי הקדירה) is apparently to convey a flat or slightly concave blade rather than a deep bucket.", 4 "The la'az appears to be Old French crochins or crochets, 'small hooks/crooks.'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:55:06.093071012Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}